   356	resolve dependencies and quantify the RUF impacts in §4. In §5,
   357	we further conduct an ecosystem-scale study on RUF and answer
   358	three research questions. We continue by discussing our RUF impact mitigation techniques and suggestions in §6. Related work is
   359	presented in §8. Lastly, we conclude the whole paper in §9.
   360	
   361	
   362	**2** **BACKGROUND**
   363	
   364	
   365	In this section, we first give preliminaries on unstable features of
   366	the Rust compiler and then discuss the cargo package manager and
   367	dependency management.
   368	
   369	
   370	**2.1** **Unstable Feature of Rust compiler**
   371	
   372	
   373	Rust compiler has three release channels: nightly, beta, and stable [ 54 ]. Developers can use any channel releases to build their
   374	projects. Especially, the nightly channel provides unstable features to extend the functionalities of the compiler. We define these
   375	features as **Rust Unstable Features** (short for **RUF** ) [ 57 ]. By
   376	adding code #![feature(feature_name)], developers can enable the
   377	RUF feature_name . Rust defines #![feature(feature_name)] as **RUF**
   378	**configuration** [ 55 ]. Figure 2a gives an example of RUF usage. Without the RUF configuration of #![feature(box_syntax)], let x =
   379	box 1 will cause a compilation failure as box cannot be resolved.
   380	Moreover, developers can also specify **configuration predicates**
   381	(like compiler_flag or target_os = "linux" ) in the RUF configuration to allow conditional compilation [ 55 ], such as specifying
   382	operating systems, as shown in Figure 2b. RUF will be enabled if
   383	the configuration predicate is true.
   384	**RUF Status.** RUF have two major types, language features and
   385	library features. The language feature is implemented in the Rust
   386	compiler to provide compiler support, such as syntax extension,
   387	user-defined compiler plugin, etc. Library feature is implemented
   388	in the Rust standard library to provide extra functionalities that are
   389	under development. Language feature has four types of statuses:
   390	
   391	- _Accepted._ The RUF is stable and integrated into the stable compiler.
   392	
   393	- _Active._ The RUF is under development and can only be used in a
   394	nightly compiler.
   395	
   396	- _Incomplete._ The RUF is incomplete and not recommended to use.
   397	
   398	- _Removed._ Not supported by the compiler anymore.
   399	
   400	Library feature only has two statuses: _stable_ and _unstable_, corresponding to _accepted_ and _active_, respectively.
   401	
   402	**RUF Impacts via Dependencies.** RUF not only impacts Rust packages directly but also affects other packages through dependencies.
   403	Packages in the Rust ecosystem often reuse other packages, thus
   404	propagating RUF impacts. For example, redox_syscall-0.1.57 uses
   405	removed RUF llvm_asm to implement syscall wrappers [ 21 ], leading
   406	to compilation failures. Even worse, any packages depending on
   407	redox_syscall-0.1.57 will get the same compilation failure. This
   408	
   409	
   410	
   411	**Figure 3: PF example.**
   412	
   413	
   414	reveals that RUF threats can be amplified through package dependencies in the ecosystem.
   415	
   416	**RUF Threats.** RUF introduces at least three potential threats to
   417	the Rust ecosystem. **1) Compilation failure.** Once the RUF is
   418	removed, all packages enabling it can no longer compile. As RUF
   419	provides functionality extensions at the compiler level, removing or
   420	replacing RUF to avoid compilation failure is not easy. **2) Unstable**
   421	**functionality.** RUF are unstable and are still under development.
   422	Therefore, the functionalities, syntax, and implementation of RUF
   423	can be changed, which may cause unstable behaviors, compilation
   424	failures, or even runtime errors [ 13 ]. Even worse, RUF can impact
   425	the Rust compiler architecture if implemented poorly, even though
   426	it’s not enabled [ 23 ]. **3) Unstable compiler selection imposed**
   427	**by RUF.** RUF force developers to use nightly compilers. As a result,
   428	all projects that depend on RUF must be compiled using nightly
   429	compilers, though developers are recommended to use stable ones.
   430	
   431	
   432	**2.2** **Cargo Package Manager and Dependency**
   433	**Management**
   434	
   435	
   436	_Cargo_ is the official package manager of Rust, which manages Rust
   437	package dependencies and collaborates with the Rust compiler
   438	to build Rust projects. It can also be used by developers to upload or download packages to/from the official package registry
   439	crates.io [ 50 ], which hosts packages for the Rust ecosystem. _Cargo_
   440	uses semantic versioning specifications to manage versions of Rust
   441	packages. The basic version format is <MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH> . MAJOR
   442	is increased when there are incompatible API changes. When there
   443	are backward-compatible changes of functionalities added, MINOR is
   444	increased. PATCH is increased only when bug fixes are introduced.
   445	
   446	**Dependency Types.** _Cargo_ has five types of dependencies [ 53 ]: 1)
   447	Normal dependency. It is used in runtime, which is the most common dependency type. 2) Build dependency. It is used in building
   448	scripts to create environments like data, code, etc. 3) Development
   449	dependency. It is used only in tests, examples, and benchmarks. 4)
   450	Target (Platform-specific) dependency. Dependency marked _tar-_
   451	_get_ is enabled only in a specif<response clipped><NOTE>To save on context only part of this file has been shown to you. You should retry this tool after you have searched inside the file with `grep -n` in order to find the line numbers of what you are looking for.</NOTE>


